<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Contrast on Typophile Archive</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/tags/contrast/</link><description>Recent content in Contrast on Typophile Archive</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Tue, 07 Oct 2014 18:16:00 +0200</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/tags/contrast/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Heading to match/contrast with Dolly</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/120281/</link><pubDate>Tue, 07 Oct 2014 18:16:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/120281/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;I&amp;rsquo;m setting an extended text in Dolly. I like it, but I don&amp;rsquo;t think it works well for headings, so I&amp;rsquo;m trying to find a suitable face for the headings.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;What I&amp;rsquo;ve been trying so far has been Fontin, which shares some of the calligraphic characteristics of Dolly that make it distinctive. However, one opinion I&amp;rsquo;ve gotten is that it doesn&amp;rsquo;t provide enough contrast, being too similar without being the same. Because Dolly is a face with strong character, it may be worth having a strongly contrasting style.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Beausite — Grotesk with contrast</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/118514/</link><pubDate>Wed, 06 Aug 2014 06:25:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/118514/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Hello everybody,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Last month, we released Beausite. A Grotesk in 3 levels of contrast and 6 weights. We hope you like it. Don&amp;rsquo;t hesitate to get our free trial fonts and test it in your designs.&lt;br&gt;
